    98	## Study 2: What a remembered dictionary costs
    99	
   100	**Evidence: checked-in historical benchmark record.** The comprehension
   101	materials under [.research/comprehension-tests](../.research/comprehension-tests/)
   102	compare full names with aliases at three levels: 5, 15, and 30 fields. Each
   103	level has 12 data items and 10 questions spanning lookup, filtering,
   104	cross-reference, aggregation, and multi-field reasoning. The earlier benchmark
   105	reported the following result when an explicit dictionary was supplied:
   106	
   107	| Level | Fields | Abbreviated | Full | Delta |
   108	| --- | ---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
   109	| 1 | 5 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
   110	| 2 | 15 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
   111	| 3 | 30 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
   112	
   113	This result is bounded. The tests and answers were produced in the same
   114	pipeline, so the useful signal is the reported delta, not a general claim about
   115	model accuracy. The benchmark was not rerun for this revision.
   116	
   117	The growing task-list example also exposes the operational risk. Once a header
   118	contains aliases such as <code>s</code>, <code>sc</code>,
   119	<code>sp</code>, and <code>st</code>, an agent answering a question about
   120	T-209 must first recover the dictionary, then find the row and its columns.
   121	The source materials identify five failure modes:
   122	
   123	1. No dictionary leaves an alias such as <code>c</code> ambiguous.
   124	2. Domain priors can conflict with a tool's chosen alias.
   125	3. Different tools can reuse the same alias for different fields.
   126	4. Partial context eviction can leave an incomplete dictionary.
   127	5. Large aggregations add attention pressure even when the dictionary is present.
   128	
   129	An explicit dictionary prevents the first failure in the historical benchmark,
   130	but it creates the discovery and retention requirement evaluated next.
   131	
   132	## Study 3: The alias session model
   133	
   134	**Evidence: model estimate, not a runtime measurement.** The
   135	[session simulator](../.research/session-simulator/simulate.py) models an
   136	agent that refreshes its alias dictionary after every <em>K</em> turns. It
   137	hard-codes the historical 85-token schema roundtrip and 5-token compact-header
   138	saving, plus a query mix and eviction schedule. Those inputs are assumptions of
   139	the model; they are not observations of Codex, Claude, MCP, or another agent
   140	host.
   141	
   142	| Model input | Value used by the simulator | Evidence boundary |
   143	| --- | ---: | --- |
   144	| Schema roundtrip | 85 tok | Historical measurement reused as a model input |
   145	| Compact alias saving per query | 5 tok | Historical fixture result reused as a model input |
   146	| Context eviction and query mix | Scenario-specific | Assumption |
   147	
   148	With those inputs, the simulator produces these estimates:
   149	
   150	| Session queries | Eviction K | Schema calls | Schema cost | Alias savings | Net |
   151	| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
   152	| 10 | 10 | 1 | 85 | 40 | -45 |
   153	| 10 | never | 1 | 85 | 40 | -45 |
   154	| 20 | 10 | 2 | 170 | 80 | -90 |
   155	| 20 | 20 | 1 | 85 | 80 | -5 |
   156	| 50 | 10 | 5 | 425 | 200 | -225 |
   157	| 50 | 50 | 1 | 85 | 200 | +115 |
   158	| 100 | 10 | 10 | 850 | 400 | -450 |
   159	| 100 | 20 | 5 | 425 | 400 | -25 |
   160	| 100 | 50 | 2 | 170 | 400 | +230 |
   161	| 100 | never | 1 | 85 | 400 | +315 |
   162	
   163	For its compact-format cases, the simulator evaluates 16 cases and reports four
   164	positive outcomes. For example, its compact scenario for 20 queries with K=20
   165	returns -5, while 50 queries with K=50 returns +115. That result supports only
   166	this conditional statement: aliases can be positive when the model's discovery
   167	cost is amortized over sufficiently many remembered queries. It does not measure
   168	a typical agent session or a transport comparison.
   169	
   170	The simple all-data-query amortization check for the model's 85-token and
   171	5-token inputs is:
   172	
   173	~~~text
   174	break_even_queries >= schema_cost / savings_per_query
   175	net_positive_queries > schema_cost / savings_per_query
   176	~~~
   177	
   178	At those inputs, 17 such data queries exactly amortize one schema roundtrip;
   179	net-positive savings begin at 18. These are derived model values, not
   180	host-independent thresholds.
   181	
   182	### Batching is a separate implementation capability
   183	
   184	The alias model should not be turned into a claim that one transport universally
   185	beats another. Still, batching changes the local execution shape in a way the
   186	current implementation can verify.
   187	
   188	**Situation:** an agent needs the status of T-209, T-210, and T-211.
   189	**Expected behavior:** this DSL accepts semicolon-separated statements and
   190	returns the results in source order.
   191	
   192	~~~text
   193	get(T-209) { status }; get(T-210) { status }; get(T-211) { status }
   194	~~~
   195	
   196	The parser and executor implement this multi-statement behavior, and the test
   197	suite covers three batched statements and compact rendering. This shows a
   198	capability of this Go DSL. It does not supply a per-call token total, prove a
   199	particular host framing cost, or establish anything about an MCP server's
   200	batching behavior.
   201	
   202	## Discussion: choose the structural optimization first
   203	
   204	The historical fixtures support a narrow structural insight: a schema-once
   205	header removes repeated field names from the rows. Aliases can only shorten
   206	that one declaration. The simulator then models whether learning the aliases
   207	pays for that fixed saving under its stated assumptions.
   208	
   209	| Priority | Optimization | Evidence in this repository | Boundary |
   210	| ---: | --- | --- | --- |
   211	| 1 | Field projection | Implementation selects requested fields | No percentage is claimed here |
   212	| 2 | Compact schema-once output | Historical fixtures report 44.5% to 46.6% versus JSON | Fixture and tokenizer specific |
   213	| 3 | Semicolon batching | Verified implementation behavior | External call savings depend on host and transport |
   214	| 4 | Presets | Implementation feature for common selections | No token saving is asserted here |
   215	| 5 | Field aliases | Historical fixtures show a fixed 5-token compact-header delta | Dictionary discovery is modeled, not newly measured |
   216	
   217	### MCP comparison: scoped conclusion
   218	
   219	The accepted evidence map separates implementation facts from transport
   220	economics:
   221	
   222	| Question | What the evidence supports | What remains unknown |
   223	| --- | --- | --- |
   224	| Can this DSL batch requests? | Yes. The [parser](../agentquery/parser.go#L318-L369) accepts semicolon-separated statements and [QueryAST](../agentquery/query.go#L30-L60) executes a batch. | The token saving for a particular host or transport. |
   225	| Is compact output schema-once? | Yes. [FormatCompact](../agentquery/format.go#L9-L62) writes a field header before its rows. | A universal percentage across tokenizers and payloads. |
   226	| Is MCP more or less token-efficient here? | No repository-local result answers this. | There is no MCP adapter, tool-definition fixture, prompt snapshot, host trace, or aligned workload. |
   227	
   228	MCP batching and discovery costs must be bounded by protocol version, SDK,
   229	server design, and host behavior. The official
   230	[Ruby SDK protocol-version reference](https://ruby.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/protocol-versions/)
   231	records that protocol version 2025-06-18 removed JSON-RPC batching, while the
   232	official [TypeScript SDK request-body reference](https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/api/@modelcontextprotocol/server/server/requestBody.html)
   233	documents a current maximum of 100 messages in a JSON-RPC batch array. Neither
   234	source guarantees that an agent host exposes or sends a batch, and neither
   235	measures prompt-token cost for this repository.
   236	
   237	**Reasoning: MCP can still be the better interface when interoperability is the
   238	requirement rather than a proven local token minimum.** A remote service that
   239	must expose model-controlled tools to several MCP-capable hosts, or an existing
   240	MCP deployment that already meets the integration requirement, can justify MCP.
   241	The [MCP server overview](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/server/index)
   242	supports that tool-interoperability rationale. It is not evidence of a token
   243	win. The failure mode is treating any of those integration benefits as a
   244	measured break-even point; a real comparison needs a server/host pair, protocol
   245	and SDK version, discovery transcript, tokenizer, and aligned workload.
   246	
   247	## Reproducing and extending the evidence
   248	
   249	The checked-in artifacts make the historical alias result inspectable:
   250	
   251	| Artifact | Purpose |
   252	| --- | --- |
   253	| [Synthetic payload generator](../.research/synthetic-payloads/generate.py) | Generates the four fixed payload scales and three formats |
   254	| [Tokenizer measurement script](../.research/synthetic-payloads/measure.py) | Counts the fixtures with <code>tiktoken</code> and <code>cl100k_base</code> |
   255	| [Comprehension materials](../.research/comprehension-tests/) | Holds the three alias/full test levels |
   256	| [Session simulator](../.research/session-simulator/simulate.py) | Evaluates the documented 16 model scenarios |
   257	
   258	Run the tokenizer measurement only in an isolated environment where
   259	<code>tiktoken</code> is installed:
   260	
   261	~~~bash
   262	python3 .research/synthetic-payloads/generate.py
   263	python3 .research/synthetic-payloads/measure.py
   264	~~~
   265	
   266	Run the model separately so its output is not presented as a measurement:
   267	
   268	~~~bash
   269	python3 .research/session-simulator/simulate.py
   270	~~~
   271	
   272	To make an MCP token claim, add a distinct, reproducible benchmark with the
   273	exact server, host, protocol/SDK version, tool definitions, prompt snapshot,
   274	tokenizer, and workload. Do not reuse the historical 346-element comparison as
   275	if those artifacts were present here.
   276	
   277	## Conclusion: the decision for this formatter
   278	
   279	**Do not add field aliases to this compact formatter on the current evidence.**
   280	The checked-in fixtures show that aliases are a one-header optimization, and
   281	the session model turns that small fixed gain into a benefit only under its
   282	explicit retention assumptions. For this formatter, aliases are a one-header
   283	optimization; no repository-local MCP benchmark establishes a transport-wide
   284	token winner.
   285	
   286	The concrete next action is therefore conditional: prioritize field projection,
   287	compact schema-once output, and the existing DSL batching capability for this
   288	formatter; choose MCP when interoperability requires it; and measure an aligned
   289	host/server workload before making any transport-token claim.
   290	
   291	## References
   292	
   293	1. [MCP Token-Economics Evidence Map](../.research/260923_mcp-token-economics-evidence.md), repository research note, September 2026.
   294	2. [MCP Ruby SDK protocol versions](https://ruby.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/protocol-versions/), official protocol-version reference.
   295	3. [MCP TypeScript SDK request body](https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/api/@modelcontextprotocol/server/server/requestBody.html), official request-body reference.
   296	4. [MCP server overview](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/server/index), official specification.

